    "lem-log-determinant-strictly-concave-on-positive-definite-matrices": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space and let $T:V\\to V$ be\nself-adjoint. Then $V$ has an orthonormal basis consisting of eigenvectors of\n$T$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-determinant-of-a-square-matrix",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring, let $n\\ge1$, and let $A=(a_{ij})\\in M_n(R)$. Its **determinant** is\n$$\\det(A):=\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_n}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)\\prod_{i<n}a_{\\sigma(i),i}.$$\nThe sum is finite because $S_n$ has $n!$ elements. The product is taken in $R$, so the signs $1$ and $-1$ act through the ring identities. The formula uses columns indexed by $i<n$ and rows indexed by $\\sigma(i)$.\n\nWhen $A$ is real, $|\\det A|$ means the ordinary real absolute value of its real determinant. It is not alternate notation for the determinant itself.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-determinant-differential-and-jacobis-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A$ be an $n\\times n$ matrix and let $H$ be a direction matrix. Then\n\n$$D\\det(A)[H]=\\operatorname{tr}(\\operatorname{adj}(A)H).$$\n\nIf $A$ is invertible, then\n\n$$D\\det(A)[H]=\\det(A)\\operatorname{tr}(A^{-1}H).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-derivative-of-matrix-inversion",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A$ be invertible. Then the inversion map is real Fr\\'echet differentiable\nat $A$, and for every direction $H$,\n\n$$D\\operatorname{inv}(A)[H]=-A^{-1}HA^{-1}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-trace-of-a-square-matrix",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a square matrix $A=(a_{ij})\\in M_n(F)$, its **trace** is the sum of its\ndiagonal entries,\n\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(A):=\\sum_{i\\in n}a_{ii}.$$\n\nFor $n=0$, this is the empty sum, so the unique empty matrix has trace $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-determinant-multiplicative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $n\\ge1$ and $A,B\\in M_n(R)$ over a commutative ring,\n$$\\det(AB)=\\det(A)\\det(B).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-determinant-of-transpose",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $n\\ge1$, every commutative ring $R$, and every $A\\in M_n(R)$,\n$$\\det(A^{\\mathsf T})=\\det(A).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-determinant-of-a-triangular-matrix",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $A=(a_{ij})\\in M_n(R)$ is upper or lower triangular over a commutative ring, with $n\\ge1$, then\n$$\\det(A)=\\prod_{i<n}a_{ii}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "d-1-1",
          "claim": "Put $H:=A_1-A_0$ and $A_s:=A_0+sH$ for $0\\le s\\le1$. For every nonzero $v$, $v^{\\mathsf T}A_sv=(1-s)v^{\\mathsf T}A_0v+s v^{\\mathsf T}A_1v>0$, so each $A_s$ is symmetric positive definite. By [F1], choose an orthogonal eigenbasis $A_s=U\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_p)U^{\\mathsf T}$ with every $\\lambda_j>0$. The determinant laws [F9], including the triangular formula for the diagonal matrix, give $\\det A_s=(\\det U)^2\\prod_j\\lambda_j$ and $(\\det U)^2=\\det(U^{\\mathsf T}U)=\\det I=1$, so $\\det A_s=\\prod_j\\lambda_j>0$. Since the determinant is a polynomial by [F2] and $\\log$ is differentiable, hence continuous, on $(0,\\infty)$ by [F4], $g(s):=\\log\\det A_s$ is real and continuous on $[0,1]$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F9",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-2-1",
          "claim": "On $0<s<1$, Jacobi's formula [F3], the scalar derivative [F4], and the chain rule [F6] give $g'(s)=\\operatorname{tr}(A_s^{-1}H)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-3-1",
          "claim": "Differentiate the expression in step 2.1 along the same affine path. The inverse derivative [F5] and linearity of the finite trace [F7] give $g''(s)=-\\operatorname{tr}(A_s^{-1}H A_s^{-1}H)$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-4-1",
          "claim": "By [F1], form the symmetric positive-definite inverse square root $A_s^{-1/2}$, and set $B:=A_s^{-1/2}HA_s^{-1/2}$. Since $H$ is symmetric, $B$ is symmetric; if $A_0\\ne A_1$, then $H\\ne0$ and invertibility of $A_s^{-1/2}$ gives $B\\ne0$. Expanding the finite trace sum and interchanging its indices gives $\\operatorname{tr}(XY)=\\operatorname{tr}(YX)$; applying this identity yields $\\operatorname{tr}(A_s^{-1}HA_s^{-1}H)=\\operatorname{tr}(B^2)=\\sum_{i,j}B_{ij}^2>0$. Thus $g''(s)<0$ for every $0<s<1$. For any $0<a<b<1$, $g'$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$ because $g$ is twice differentiable on $(0,1)$. By [F8] applied to $g'$, there is $c\\in(a,b)$ with $g'(b)-g'(a)=g''(c)(b-a)<0$. Hence $g'$ is strictly decreasing on $(0,1)$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-5-1",
          "claim": "For $0<t<1$, step 1.1 shows that $g$ is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1)$, so [F8] applies on both $[0,t]$ and $[t,1]$. Their secant slopes are $g'(a)$ and $g'(b)$ for some $a\\in(0,t)$ and $b\\in(t,1)$. Step 4.1 makes $g'$ strictly decreasing, so the first slope is strictly larger than the second; rearranging yields $g(t)>(1-t)g(0)+tg(1)$. If $A_0=A_1$, the path is constant and equality holds.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F8",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
