    "thm-finite-gaussian-component-density-normalized": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-gaussian-mixture",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $p\\ge1$, $\\mu\\in\\mathbb R^p$, and let $\\Sigma\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times p}$ be symmetric positive definite, meaning $v^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma v>0$ for every nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^p$. Define the component function\n\n$$\\phi_p(x;\\mu,\\Sigma):=(2\\pi)^{-p/2}(\\det\\Sigma)^{-1/2}\\exp\\!\\left(-\\tfrac12(x-\\mu)^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma^{-1}(x-\\mu)\\right),\\qquad x\\in\\mathbb R^p.$$\n\nThe component law is $N_p(\\mu,\\Sigma)$, the $p$-dimensional normal law with mean $\\mu$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$. The separate local normalization result proves that $\\phi_p$ is its Lebesgue density.\n\nA finite $K$-component Gaussian mixture has an integer $K\\ge1$, weights $\\pi_1,\\ldots,\\pi_K>0$ with $\\sum_{k=1}^K\\pi_k=1$, means $\\mu_k\\in\\mathbb R^p$, and symmetric positive-definite covariance matrices $\\Sigma_k\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times p}$. Its density is\n\n$$f_\\theta(x):=\\sum_{k=1}^K\\pi_k\\phi_p(x;\\mu_k,\\Sigma_k),$$\n\nwhere $\\theta=((\\pi_k,\\mu_k,\\Sigma_k))_{k=1}^K$. For a positive integer $n$ and an ordered sample $x_1,\\ldots,x_n$ modeled as conditionally independent observations from this mixture, define the likelihood $L(\\theta;x_{1:n}):=\\prod_{i=1}^n f_\\theta(x_i)$ and log likelihood $\\ell(\\theta;x_{1:n}):=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\log f_\\theta(x_i)$. The responsibility weight of component $k$ for observation $x_i$ is\n\n$$\\gamma_{ik}(\\theta):=\\frac{\\pi_k\\phi_p(x_i;\\mu_k,\\Sigma_k)}{\\sum_{j=1}^K\\pi_j\\phi_p(x_i;\\mu_j,\\Sigma_j)}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-standard-normal-and-normal-laws",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Define $\\gamma(E)=\\int_E e^{-x^2/2}/\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,dx$ for Borel E in $\\mathbb R$. By [[lem-normal-density-has-total-mass-one]] and [[thm-indefinite-integral-of-a-nonnegative-function-is-a-measure]], gamma is a probability measure; denote it $N(0,1)$. For $m\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\sigma\\ge0$, define $N(m,\\sigma^2)$ as the law of $x\\mapsto m+\\sigma x$ on $(\\mathbb R,\\mathcal B,\\gamma)$. This affine map is continuous: for $\\sigma$>0 choose $\\delta$=$\\varepsilon$/$\\sigma$, and for $\\sigma$=0 it is constant. Its inverse images of opens are open, so it is Borel measurable. [[lem-law-of-a-random-element-is-a-probability-measure]] makes its pushforward a probability. When $\\sigma$=0, the preimage of E is all of R if m belongs to E and empty otherwise, so $N(m,0)=\\delta_m$ in [[def-dirac-measure]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-multivariate-normal-law",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC and let $d\\ge1$ be finite. For $m\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and a real symmetric positive semidefinite matrix $\\Sigma$, a Borel probability law is denoted $N_d(m,\\Sigma)$ when a vector X with that law satisfies $u\\cdot X\\sim N(u\\cdot m,u^T\\Sigma u)$ for every $u\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Such a law exists, has mean m and covariance Sigma, and can be realized as $m+\\Sigma^{1/2}Z$ with independent standard-normal coordinates. Singular Sigma is allowed. Uniqueness will be proved in the following characteristic-function lemma.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-independent-random-elements-have-product-joint-law",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, and let\n$X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ for $i<n$ be independent\nrandom elements. Define\n\n$$X=(X_0,\\dots,X_{n-1}):\\Omega\\to\\prod_{i<n}S_i.$$\n\nThen $X$ is a random element of\n$\\left(\\prod_{i<n}S_i,\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\Sigma_i\\right)$, and its law is the\nfinite product of the marginal laws:\n\n$$\\mathbb P_X=\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\mathbb P_{X_i}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-lebesgue-product-measure-agrees-with-euclidean-lebesgue-on-borel-sets",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$m,n \\ge 1$. Under the identification\n$$\\mathbb R^{m+n}=\\mathbb R^m \\times \\mathbb R^n,$$\nthe product measure $\\lambda_m \\times \\lambda_n$ and the Euclidean Lebesgue\nmeasure $\\lambda_{m+n}$ agree on every Borel subset of $\\mathbb R^{m+n}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-measure-uniqueness-on-a-sigma-finite-pi-system",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal P$ be a pi-system on $X$ generating $\\mathcal A$, and let $\\mu,\\nu$ be measures on $(X,\\mathcal A)$ that agree on $\\mathcal P$. Suppose there is an increasing sequence $(P_n)$ in $\\mathcal P$ with\n\n$$X=\\bigcup_nP_n,\\qquad \\mu(P_n)=\\nu(P_n)<+\\infty\\quad(n\\in\\mathbb N).$$\n\nThen $\\mu=\\nu$ on $\\mathcal A$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space and let $T:V\\to V$ be\nself-adjoint. Then $V$ has an orthonormal basis consisting of eigenvectors of\n$T$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-lebesgue-measurable-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$U,V \\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $T : U \\to V$ be a\n$C^1$ diffeomorphism. For every nonnegative Lebesgue measurable\n$f : V \\to [0,\\infty]$,\n$$ \\int_V f(y)\\,d\\lambda_n(y) = \\int_U f(T(x))\\,|\\det DT(x)|\\,d\\lambda_n(x). $$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-linear-change-of-variables-for-lebesgue-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\ge 1$, **assume the Axiom of Countable Choice**\n([[def-countable-choice]]) and let $T : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ be linear\nwith matrix $A$ ([[def-linear-map]],\n[[lem-euclidean-linear-maps-have-matrices-and-are-bounded]]).\n\n1. **Invertible case.** If $\\det A \\ne 0$, then $T[E]$ is Lebesgue measurable for\n   every Lebesgue measurable $E$ and\n   $$\\lambda_n\\big(T[E]\\big) \\;=\\; |\\det A|\\;\\lambda_n(E),$$\n   both sides possibly $+\\infty$; the product is defined in\n   $\\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$ because $|\\det A| > 0$.\n2. **Singular case.** If $\\det A = 0$, then $T[E]$ is Lebesgue measurable with\n   $\\lambda_n(T[E]) = 0$ for **every** $E \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$.\n\n**The singular clause is stated as nullity and not as a product.** When\n$\\det A = 0$ and $\\lambda_n(E) = +\\infty$ the expression\n$|\\det A|\\,\\lambda_n(E)$ is $0\\cdot(+\\infty)$, which [[def-extended-reals]]\nleaves undefined; writing the conclusion as $\\lambda_n(T[E]) = 0$ says the same\nthing wherever the product is defined and remains a statement where it is not.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "def-determinant-of-a-square-matrix",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring, let $n\\ge1$, and let $A=(a_{ij})\\in M_n(R)$. Its **determinant** is\n$$\\det(A):=\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_n}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)\\prod_{i<n}a_{\\sigma(i),i}.$$\nThe sum is finite because $S_n$ has $n!$ elements. The product is taken in $R$, so the signs $1$ and $-1$ act through the ring identities. The formula uses columns indexed by $i<n$ and rows indexed by $\\sigma(i)$.\n\nWhen $A$ is real, $|\\det A|$ means the ordinary real absolute value of its real determinant. It is not alternate notation for the determinant itself.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "thm-determinant-multiplicative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $n\\ge1$ and $A,B\\in M_n(R)$ over a commutative ring,\n$$\\det(AB)=\\det(A)\\det(B).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "thm-determinant-of-transpose",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $n\\ge1$, every commutative ring $R$, and every $A\\in M_n(R)$,\n$$\\det(A^{\\mathsf T})=\\det(A).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "thm-determinant-of-a-triangular-matrix",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $A=(a_{ij})\\in M_n(R)$ is upper or lower triangular over a commutative ring, with $n\\ge1$, then\n$$\\det(A)=\\prod_{i<n}a_{ii}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "lem-ac-supplies-sequential-choices-for-probability-constructions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Then countable choice holds. Moreover, if $R\\subseteq X\\times X$ is serial on a nonempty set X and $a\\in X$, there is a sequence $(x_n)_{n\\ge0}$ with $x_0=a$ and $x_n R x_{n+1}$ for every n. Thus the countable and dependent choices required by the probability-product suppliers are available under AC.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "d-1-1",
          "claim": "Set $A:=\\Sigma^{1/2}$, the symmetric positive-definite square root in the Statement, so $A^2=\\Sigma$. By [F8], write $A=U\\operatorname{diag}(a_1,\\ldots,a_p)U^{\\mathsf T}$ with $U^{\\mathsf T}U=I$ and $a_j>0$. The determinant laws [F11], including the triangular formula for the diagonal matrix, give $(\\det U)^2=\\det(U^{\\mathsf T}U)=\\det I=1$ and $\\det A=(\\det U)^2\\prod_j a_j=\\prod_j a_j>0$. Also $\\det\\Sigma=\\det(A^2)=(\\det A)^2$, so $\\det A=\\sqrt{\\det\\Sigma}$. In the same basis, $\\Sigma^{-1}=A^{-2}=U\\operatorname{diag}(a_1^{-2},\\ldots,a_p^{-2})U^{\\mathsf T}$ and $A^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma^{-1}A=I$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "F8",
            "F11",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-1-2",
          "claim": "By [F2] and [F4], the joint law of $Z$ is the product of its standard-normal marginals. Define $g(y):=\\prod_{j=1}^p\\phi_1(y_j)=(2\\pi)^{-p/2}e^{-\\|y\\|_2^2/2}$. Tonelli [F5] and the product-measure identity [F6] give $\\int_{\\mathbb R^p}g\\,d\\lambda_p=\\prod_{j=1}^p\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\phi_1\\,d\\lambda_1=1$ and, for every Borel rectangle $R=\\prod_{j=1}^p B_j$, $\\int_R g\\,d\\lambda_p=\\prod_{j=1}^p\\int_{B_j}\\phi_1\\,d\\lambda_1$. Thus $g\\,d\\lambda_p$ and the product law of $Z$ are probability measures agreeing on rectangles. The rational half-open rectangles form a generating pi-system; $(-N,N]^p$ is an increasing exhaustion from it, and the common rectangle values make their measures equal and finite on every such box. Hence [F7] identifies the measures. AC supplies countable choice for the product-Lebesgue identity [F6].",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F12",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $T(y)=\\mu+Ay$. By step 1.1, $A$ is invertible, so $T$ is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism of $\\mathbb R^p$ with $|\\det DT|=\\det A$. The linear measure-scaling result [F10] confirms this is the Lebesgue volume factor. The displayed function $\\phi_p$ is continuous and positive, so $f(x)=\\mathbf1_E(x)\\phi_p(x;\\mu,\\Sigma)$ is nonnegative Borel for every Borel $E\\subseteq\\mathbb R^p$. AC supplies the countable-choice assumption of [F9] by [F12]. Apply [F9] to $f$ and the change of variables by $T$ to obtain $\\int_E\\phi_p(x;\\mu,\\Sigma)\\,dx=\\int_{T^{-1}(E)}\\phi_p(T(y);\\mu,\\Sigma)\\det A\\,dy.$ For $x=T(y)$, $x-\\mu=Ay$ and $A^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma^{-1}A=A A^{-2}A=I$. Thus the exponent in [F1] becomes $-\\|y\\|_2^2/2$, while $(\\det\\Sigma)^{-1/2}\\det A=1$ by step 1.1. Hence $\\phi_p(T(y);\\mu,\\Sigma)\\det A=g(y)$. The last integral equals $\\int_{T^{-1}(E)}g(y)\\,dy=\\mathbb P(X\\in E)$ by step 1.2 and the definition of $X$. Thus $\\phi_p$ is the Lebesgue density of $X$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "F12",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-3-1",
          "claim": "By [F3], $X$ has law $N_p(\\mu,\\Sigma)$. Step 2.1 identifies that law on every Borel set with integration against $\\phi_p$; taking $E=\\mathbb R^p$ gives $\\int_{\\mathbb R^p}\\phi_p(x;\\mu,\\Sigma)\\,dx=\\mathbb P(X\\in\\mathbb R^p)=1$. Nonnegativity follows directly from [F1] and $\\det\\Sigma>0$ in step 1.1.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
