    "thm-gaussian-mixture-exact-m-step-maximizes-q": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-gaussian-mixture",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $p\\ge1$, $\\mu\\in\\mathbb R^p$, and let $\\Sigma\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times p}$ be symmetric positive definite, meaning $v^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma v>0$ for every nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^p$. Define the component function\n\n$$\\phi_p(x;\\mu,\\Sigma):=(2\\pi)^{-p/2}(\\det\\Sigma)^{-1/2}\\exp\\!\\left(-\\tfrac12(x-\\mu)^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma^{-1}(x-\\mu)\\right),\\qquad x\\in\\mathbb R^p.$$\n\nThe component law is $N_p(\\mu,\\Sigma)$, the $p$-dimensional normal law with mean $\\mu$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$. The separate local normalization result proves that $\\phi_p$ is its Lebesgue density.\n\nA finite $K$-component Gaussian mixture has an integer $K\\ge1$, weights $\\pi_1,\\ldots,\\pi_K>0$ with $\\sum_{k=1}^K\\pi_k=1$, means $\\mu_k\\in\\mathbb R^p$, and symmetric positive-definite covariance matrices $\\Sigma_k\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times p}$. Its density is\n\n$$f_\\theta(x):=\\sum_{k=1}^K\\pi_k\\phi_p(x;\\mu_k,\\Sigma_k),$$\n\nwhere $\\theta=((\\pi_k,\\mu_k,\\Sigma_k))_{k=1}^K$. For a positive integer $n$ and an ordered sample $x_1,\\ldots,x_n$ modeled as conditionally independent observations from this mixture, define the likelihood $L(\\theta;x_{1:n}):=\\prod_{i=1}^n f_\\theta(x_i)$ and log likelihood $\\ell(\\theta;x_{1:n}):=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\log f_\\theta(x_i)$. The responsibility weight of component $k$ for observation $x_i$ is\n\n$$\\gamma_{ik}(\\theta):=\\frac{\\pi_k\\phi_p(x_i;\\mu_k,\\Sigma_k)}{\\sum_{j=1}^K\\pi_j\\phi_p(x_i;\\mu_j,\\Sigma_j)}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-finite-gaussian-component-density-normalized",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $p\\ge1$, let $\\mu\\in\\mathbb R^p$, and let $\\Sigma\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times p}$ be symmetric positive definite. Let $Z_1,\\ldots,Z_p$ be independent standard normal random variables, let $Z=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_p)^{\\mathsf T}$, and set $X=\\mu+\\Sigma^{1/2}Z$, where $\\Sigma^{1/2}$ is the symmetric positive-definite square root. Then the law of $X$ has Lebesgue density\n\n$$\\phi_p(x;\\mu,\\Sigma)=(2\\pi)^{-p/2}(\\det\\Sigma)^{-1/2}\\exp\\!\\left(-\\tfrac12(x-\\mu)^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma^{-1}(x-\\mu)\\right),\\qquad x\\in\\mathbb R^p.$$\n\nIn particular, $\\phi_p$ is nonnegative and $\\int_{\\mathbb R^p}\\phi_p(x;\\mu,\\Sigma)\\,dx=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-probability-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **probability measure** on a measurable space $(X,\\mathcal A)$ is a measure $\\mathbb P$ with $\\mathbb P(X)=1$ ([[def-measure]]). The triple $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mathbb P)$ is a **probability space** ([[def-measure-space]]); $X$ is its sample space and the members of $\\mathcal A$ are its events.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-kullback-leibler-divergence",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $P,Q$ be probability measures on the same measurable space\n([[def-probability-measure]]). Absolute continuity $P\\ll Q$ has the convention\nof [[def-absolutely-continuous-with-respect-to-a-positive-measure]]. If\n$P\\not\\ll Q$, define $D(P\\|Q)=+\\infty$. If $P\\ll Q$, assume AC\n([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) as in [[def-likelihood-ratio-as-rn-derivative]] and\nlet $r=dP/dQ$ be a finite-valued nonnegative measurable representative of its\nRadon–Nikodym class; such a representative exists by\n[[lem-radon-nikodym-density-of-a-finite-positive-measure-has-a-nonnegative-representative]].\nOn $\\{r=0\\}$ set $\\ell=0$, and on $\\{r>0\\}$ set $\\ell=\\log r$. This\nfunction is measurable: for $a\\ge0$, $\\{\\ell>a\\}=\\{r>\\exp(a)\\}$, and for\n$a<0$, $\\{\\ell>a\\}=\\{r=0\\}\\cup\\{r>\\exp(a)\\}$, by the inverse and\nmonotonicity properties of the natural logarithm\n([[def-natural-logarithm]], [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]]). These are\nmeasurable sets by [[def-extended-real-valued-measurable-function]]. Since\n$P(\\{r=0\\})=\\int_{\\{r=0\\}}r\\,dQ=0$ by\n[[thm-likelihood-ratio-change-of-measure]], the value chosen there does not\naffect $\\int\\ell\\,dP$. Define\n$$D(P\\|Q):=\\int \\ell\\,dP,$$\nwhere $\\log$ is the natural logarithm ([[def-natural-logarithm]]).\n\nThe definition is independent of the representative: two representatives\nagree $Q$-almost everywhere, hence $P$-almost everywhere. Put\n$$\\psi(0):=0,\\qquad \\psi(t):=t\\log t\\quad(t>0).$$\nThe function $\\psi$ is continuous at zero: for $s\\ge1$,\n$$0\\le \\frac{\\log s}{s}=\\frac1s\\int_1^s\\frac{du}{u}\\le\\frac1s\\int_1^s\\frac{du}{\\sqrt u}\\le\\frac2{\\sqrt s}.$$\nThus $t\\log t\\to0$ as $t\\downarrow0$; here $L=\\log$ by\n[[cor-integral-logarithm-agrees-with-natural-logarithm]] and\n[[def-integral-logarithm]]. On $(0,\\infty)$, $\\psi$ is a product of continuous\nfunctions by [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]] and\n[[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]], so $\\psi$ is continuous on\n$[0,\\infty)$ and $\\psi(r)$ is measurable by\n[[def-extended-real-valued-measurable-function]]. Its signed integral is\ndefined by the positive and negative parts\n([[def-positive-and-negative-parts-of-a-function]],\n[[def-nonnegative-lebesgue-integral]]).\n\nFor $0<t\\le1$, the function $-t\\log t$ has derivative $-\\log t-1$ and\nmaximum $1/e$ at $t=1/e$: $e=\\exp(1)>1$ by\n[[def-real-exponential-function-and-e]] and\n[[thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing]], $1/e=\\exp(-1)$ by\n[[cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity]], and $\\log(1/e)=-1$ by\n[[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]] and the inverse definition. The mean value\ntheorem gives increase before and decrease after this point\n([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[thm-integral-logarithm-derivative-and-normalisation]],\n[[cor-integral-logarithm-agrees-with-natural-logarithm]], [[cor-mean-value-theorem]]).\nAt $t=0$ the negative part is zero. Since $Q$ is a probability measure,\n$$\\int \\psi(r)^-\\,dQ\\le\\frac1e.$$\nThe change-of-measure identity of [[thm-likelihood-ratio-change-of-measure]],\napplied to $\\ell^-$ gives $\\int\\ell^-\\,dP=\\int\\psi(r)^-\\,dQ\\le1/e$,\nso $\\int\\ell\\,dP$ is well-defined; applying it to both positive and negative\nparts gives\n$$D(P\\|Q)=\\int\\ell\\,dP=\\int\\psi(r)\\,dQ=\\int r\\log r\\,dQ,$$\nwith $0\\log0:=0$ and a finite negative part. Therefore the extended integral\nis well-defined without an $\\infty-\\infty$ ambiguity.\n\nThis convention is directional: the first argument must be absolutely\ncontinuous with respect to the second for the integral formula to apply.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-kullback-leibler-is-nonnegative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC and use the directional convention of\n[[def-kullback-leibler-divergence]]. For probability measures $P,Q$ on one\nmeasurable space, $D(P\\|Q)\\ge0$, and $D(P\\|Q)=0$ if and only if $P=Q$.\n\nThere is also the following normalized-density identity. Let\n$(\\mathsf X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a sigma-finite measure space: it is a set\nwith a sigma-algebra and measure ([[def-measure-space]]), and there is a\ncountable measurable cover by sets of finite $\\mu$-measure\n([[def-finite-sigma-finite-and-semifinite-measures]]). Let\n$\\gamma:\\mathsf X\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a finite-valued measurable function with\n$Z:=\\int\\gamma\\,d\\mu\\in(0,\\infty)$, and put $\\pi=\\gamma/Z$. Let\n$q:\\mathsf X\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a finite-valued measurable probability density,\nso $\\int q\\,d\\mu=1$, and suppose $q=0$ $\\mu$-almost everywhere on\n$\\{\\gamma=0\\}$. Assume\n$$\\int q\\bigl(|\\log\\gamma|+|\\log q|\\bigr)\\,d\\mu<\\infty,$$\nwhere each term with $q=0$ is defined to be zero. Then\n$$\\log Z =\\int q(\\log\\gamma-\\log q)\\,d\\mu +D(q\\,\\mu\\|\\pi\\,\\mu).$$\nIn particular, the first integral is at most $\\log Z$. This identity concerns\nnormalized densities only; it does not assert posterior existence for an\nunspecified statistical model.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-log-determinant-strictly-concave-on-positive-definite-matrices",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $p\\ge1$ and let $A_0,A_1\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times p}$ be symmetric positive-definite matrices. For $0<t<1$ and $A_0\\ne A_1$,\n\n$$\\log\\det((1-t)A_0+tA_1)>(1-t)\\log\\det A_0+t\\log\\det A_1.$$\n\nIf $A_0=A_1$, equality holds. Thus $A\\mapsto\\log\\det A$ is strictly concave on the positive-definite cone.",
          "uses": [
            "3.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-determinant-differential-and-jacobis-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A$ be an $n\\times n$ matrix and let $H$ be a direction matrix. Then\n\n$$D\\det(A)[H]=\\operatorname{tr}(\\operatorname{adj}(A)H).$$\n\nIf $A$ is invertible, then\n\n$$D\\det(A)[H]=\\det(A)\\operatorname{tr}(A^{-1}H).$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "def-trace-of-a-square-matrix",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a square matrix $A=(a_{ij})\\in M_n(F)$, its **trace** is the sum of its\ndiagonal entries,\n\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(A):=\\sum_{i\\in n}a_{ii}.$$\n\nFor $n=0$, this is the empty sum, so the unique empty matrix has trace $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.3",
            "3.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "def-determinant-of-a-square-matrix",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring, let $n\\ge1$, and let $A=(a_{ij})\\in M_n(R)$. Its **determinant** is\n$$\\det(A):=\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_n}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)\\prod_{i<n}a_{\\sigma(i),i}.$$\nThe sum is finite because $S_n$ has $n!$ elements. The product is taken in $R$, so the signs $1$ and $-1$ act through the ring identities. The formula uses columns indexed by $i<n$ and rows indexed by $\\sigma(i)$.\n\nWhen $A$ is real, $|\\det A|$ means the ordinary real absolute value of its real determinant. It is not alternate notation for the determinant itself.",
          "uses": [
            "3.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "thm-determinant-multiplicative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $n\\ge1$ and $A,B\\in M_n(R)$ over a commutative ring,\n$$\\det(AB)=\\det(A)\\det(B).$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space and let $T:V\\to V$ be\nself-adjoint. Then $V$ has an orthonormal basis consisting of eigenvectors of\n$T$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.2",
            "3.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "3.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "3.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "d-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1] and [F2], every numerator and denominator in $\\gamma_{ik}$ is finite and strictly positive. Hence $\\gamma_{ik}>0$. Summing the responsibility formula over $k$ gives $\\sum_k\\gamma_{ik}=1$, so $N_k>0$ and $\\sum_kN_k=n$. Thus all displayed weighted means and scatters are defined.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-2-1",
          "claim": "For each nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^p$, $v^{\\mathsf T}S_kv=N_k^{-1}\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}(v^{\\mathsf T}(x_i-\\bar x_k))^2$. If it were zero, positivity of each $\\gamma_{ik}$ would force $v^{\\mathsf T}x_i=v^{\\mathsf T}\\bar x_k$ for every $i$. That would put all observations in the proper affine hyperplane $\\{x:v^{\\mathsf T}x=v^{\\mathsf T}\\bar x_k\\}$, contradicting their full affine span. Since $S_k$ is visibly symmetric, it is positive definite.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-2-2",
          "claim": "Expanding the component density in [F1,F2] in the definition of $Q$ gives $Q(\\vartheta\\mid\\theta)=\\sum_kN_k\\log\\rho_k-\\frac{np}{2}\\log(2\\pi)-\\frac12\\sum_k\\left(N_k\\log\\det V_k+\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}(x_i-m_k)^{\\mathsf T}V_k^{-1}(x_i-m_k)\\right).$ Here [F13] supplies $\\log(\\exp u)=u$ and the product and reciprocal laws. For each positive $a$, $\\log(a^{-1/2})=-\\frac12\\log a$ follows by applying those laws to $(a^{-1/2})^2=1/a$; applying the product law $p$ times gives $\\log((2\\pi)^{-p/2})=-\\frac p2\\log(2\\pi)$. All logarithms are finite: weights are positive, and [F2] gives finite positive component densities for each positive-definite covariance, so their determinants are positive.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F13",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-3-1",
          "claim": "Define $\\alpha_k=N_k/n$, and for each subset $B\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,K\\}$ set $P(B):=\\sum_{k\\in B}\\alpha_k$ and $R(B):=\\sum_{k\\in B}\\rho_k$. Finite sums make these set functions additive on disjoint subsets; a disjoint sequence has at most $K$ nonempty members, so they are countably additive. Step 1.1 and the candidate weight constraint give total mass one, hence [F3] makes $P,R$ probability measures. Each $\\rho_k>0$, so $P\\ll R$. For every subset $B$, $\\sum_{k\\in B}(\\alpha_k/\\rho_k)\\rho_k=P(B)$, hence $dP/dR(k)=\\alpha_k/\\rho_k$. The KL definition [F4] therefore gives $D(P\\|R)=\\sum_k\\alpha_k\\log(\\alpha_k/\\rho_k)\\ge0$ by [A1,F5]. The weight-dependent part of $Q$ at $\\alpha$ minus that at $\\rho$ is $\\sum_kN_k\\log(\\alpha_k/\\rho_k)=nD(P\\|R)\\ge0$, with equality only when $P=R$, hence $\\rho_k=\\alpha_k$ for every $k$ by [F5].",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-3-2",
          "claim": "For fixed candidate covariance $V_k$, write $d_i=x_i-\\bar x_k$ and $e=\\bar x_k-m_k$. Since $\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}d_i=0$, expanding the quadratic form gives $\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}(x_i-m_k)^{\\mathsf T}V_k^{-1}(x_i-m_k)=\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}d_i^{\\mathsf T}V_k^{-1}d_i+N_ke^{\\mathsf T}V_k^{-1}e.$ The last term is nonnegative and is zero exactly when $m_k=\\bar x_k$, because $V_k^{-1}$ is positive definite by [F12]. It occurs with a negative coefficient in $Q$ by step 2.2, so the unique maximizing mean is $\\bar x_k$.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F12",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-3-3",
          "claim": "At this mean, coordinate expansion of the finite trace gives $\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}d_i^{\\mathsf T}V_k^{-1}d_i=N_k\\operatorname{tr}(V_k^{-1}S_k)$. Therefore the covariance-dependent part of $Q$ is $-\\frac{N_k}{2}(\\log\\det V_k+\\operatorname{tr}(V_k^{-1}S_k))$ by step 2.2. Set $A=V_k^{-1}$; determinant identity [F11], the logarithm reciprocal law [F13], and the finite trace identity [F10] rewrite this as $\\frac{N_k}{2}(\\log\\det A-\\operatorname{tr}(AS_k))$. The candidate $A_*=S_k^{-1}$ is symmetric positive definite by step 2.1 and [F12]. Conversely, every symmetric positive-definite $A$ has positive-definite inverse, so this reparameterization ranges over the full covariance domain.",
          "step": "3.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F10",
            "F11",
            "F12",
            "F13",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-3-4",
          "claim": "Fix any other positive-definite $A$ and put $H=A-A_*$, $A_t=A_*+tH$, and $h(t)=\\log\\det A_t-\\operatorname{tr}(A_tS_k)$. Each $A_t$ is positive definite by convexity of the cone. By [F6], $h$ is strictly concave when $H\\ne0$. Jacobi's formula [F7], the scalar logarithm derivative [F8], and the chain rule [F9] give $h'(0)=\\operatorname{tr}(A_*^{-1}H)-\\operatorname{tr}(HS_k)=0$, since $A_*^{-1}=S_k$ and [F10] permits cyclic rearrangement of the finite trace. If $h(1)\\ge h(0)$, strict concavity gives $h(s)>h(0)$ for each $0<s<1$. For $0<u<s$, concavity gives $(h(u)-h(0))/u\\ge(h(s)-h(0))/s>0$. Letting $u\\downarrow0$ contradicts $h'(0)=0$. Hence $h(1)<h(0)$ for every $A\\ne A_*$, so $V_k=S_k$ is the unique maximizing covariance.",
          "step": "3.4",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-4-1",
          "claim": "The expression for $Q$ is a sum over components of a weight term, a mean-dependent quadratic term, and a covariance-dependent term, with no cross-component parameter terms. Steps 3.1, 3.2, and 3.4 show each is uniquely maximized by the displayed parameters; step 2.1 ensures each maximizing covariance remains in the permitted positive-definite domain. The product choice is therefore an admissible attained global maximizer, and equality forces every component parameter to equal the displayed update formulas. Thus the displayed E-step responsibilities and M-step formulas are well-defined and attain the claimed maximum, without using a procedure-level property.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 3.2",
            "step 3.4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
