    "thm-gaussian-mixture-em-observed-likelihood-is-nondecreasing": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-gaussian-mixture",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $p\\ge1$, $\\mu\\in\\mathbb R^p$, and let $\\Sigma\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times p}$ be symmetric positive definite, meaning $v^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma v>0$ for every nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^p$. Define the component function\n\n$$\\phi_p(x;\\mu,\\Sigma):=(2\\pi)^{-p/2}(\\det\\Sigma)^{-1/2}\\exp\\!\\left(-\\tfrac12(x-\\mu)^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma^{-1}(x-\\mu)\\right),\\qquad x\\in\\mathbb R^p.$$\n\nThe component law is $N_p(\\mu,\\Sigma)$, the $p$-dimensional normal law with mean $\\mu$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$. The separate local normalization result proves that $\\phi_p$ is its Lebesgue density.\n\nA finite $K$-component Gaussian mixture has an integer $K\\ge1$, weights $\\pi_1,\\ldots,\\pi_K>0$ with $\\sum_{k=1}^K\\pi_k=1$, means $\\mu_k\\in\\mathbb R^p$, and symmetric positive-definite covariance matrices $\\Sigma_k\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times p}$. Its density is\n\n$$f_\\theta(x):=\\sum_{k=1}^K\\pi_k\\phi_p(x;\\mu_k,\\Sigma_k),$$\n\nwhere $\\theta=((\\pi_k,\\mu_k,\\Sigma_k))_{k=1}^K$. For a positive integer $n$ and an ordered sample $x_1,\\ldots,x_n$ modeled as conditionally independent observations from this mixture, define the likelihood $L(\\theta;x_{1:n}):=\\prod_{i=1}^n f_\\theta(x_i)$ and log likelihood $\\ell(\\theta;x_{1:n}):=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\log f_\\theta(x_i)$. The responsibility weight of component $k$ for observation $x_i$ is\n\n$$\\gamma_{ik}(\\theta):=\\frac{\\pi_k\\phi_p(x_i;\\mu_k,\\Sigma_k)}{\\sum_{j=1}^K\\pi_j\\phi_p(x_i;\\mu_j,\\Sigma_j)}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.2",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-finite-gaussian-component-density-normalized",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $p\\ge1$, let $\\mu\\in\\mathbb R^p$, and let $\\Sigma\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times p}$ be symmetric positive definite. Let $Z_1,\\ldots,Z_p$ be independent standard normal random variables, let $Z=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_p)^{\\mathsf T}$, and set $X=\\mu+\\Sigma^{1/2}Z$, where $\\Sigma^{1/2}$ is the symmetric positive-definite square root. Then the law of $X$ has Lebesgue density\n\n$$\\phi_p(x;\\mu,\\Sigma)=(2\\pi)^{-p/2}(\\det\\Sigma)^{-1/2}\\exp\\!\\left(-\\tfrac12(x-\\mu)^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma^{-1}(x-\\mu)\\right),\\qquad x\\in\\mathbb R^p.$$\n\nIn particular, $\\phi_p$ is nonnegative and $\\int_{\\mathbb R^p}\\phi_p(x;\\mu,\\Sigma)\\,dx=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-latent-variable-joint-and-observed-data-factorization",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Theta,\\mathcal T)$, $(\\mathsf X,\\mathcal A)$, and\n$(\\mathsf Z,\\mathcal C)$ be measurable spaces (sets equipped with\nsigma-algebras; [[def-measurable-space]]). Let $\\nu$ and $\\mu$ be sigma-finite\nreference measures on the observation and latent spaces; each has a countable\nmeasurable cover by sets of finite measure\n([[def-finite-sigma-finite-and-semifinite-measures]]). The finite product\nsigma-algebra is generated by measurable rectangles\n([[def-product-sigma-algebra-and-finite-product-sigma-algebras]]). The product\nreference measure $\\nu\\otimes\\mu$ is the one supplied by\n[[thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique]].\n\nA **jointly measurable latent-variable density** is a specified function\n$$p:\\Theta\\times\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Z\\longrightarrow[0,\\infty]$$\nmeasurable for $\\mathcal T\\otimes\\mathcal A\\otimes\\mathcal C$ and normalized\non every parameter fibre:\n$$\\int_{\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Z}p(\\theta,x,z)\\, (\\nu\\otimes\\mu)(d(x,z))=1\\qquad(\\theta\\in\\Theta).$$\nHere $x$ is the observed coordinate and $z$ the latent coordinate. The\nobserved marginal density is\n$$m(\\theta,x):=\\int_{\\mathsf Z}p(\\theta,x,z)\\,\\mu(dz).$$\nThe valid-fibre set is\n$V:=\\{(\\theta,x):0<m(\\theta,x)<\\infty\\}$. Only on $V$ define the normalized\nlatent-fibre density\n$$q(z\\mid\\theta,x):=\\frac{p(\\theta,x,z)}{m(\\theta,x)}.$$\nThis definition requires a single jointly measurable density as an input.\n[[def-likelihood-density]] supplies neither a sigma-algebra on $\\Theta$ nor\njoint parameter measurability from separately specified likelihood sections;\nthe joint measurability above is an additional hypothesis. Because $p$ is\nalready supplied as one joint function, this definition uses no choice axiom.\nIt does not assert that AC can turn separately specified pointwise likelihood\nversions into a jointly measurable version. No quotient or pointwise\nconditional density is asserted on a zero or infinite marginal fibre.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-observed-density-is-latent-marginal-under-tonelli",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the hypotheses of\n[[def-latent-variable-joint-and-observed-data-factorization]]. Then\n$m(\\theta,x)=\\int p(\\theta,x,z)\\,\\mu(dz)$ is jointly measurable in\n$(\\theta,x)$. For each fixed $\\theta$, it is an observed-data probability\ndensity: it is $\\mathcal A$-measurable and\n$$\\int_{\\mathsf X}m(\\theta,x)\\,\\nu(dx)=1.$$\nConsequently $m(\\theta,x)<\\infty$ for $\\nu$-almost every $x$. On every valid\nfibre $0<m(\\theta,x)<\\infty$, $q(z\\mid\\theta,x)=p(\\theta,x,z)/m(\\theta,x)$ is\na measurable $\\mu$-density of total mass one. Finally, if $h:\\Theta\\times\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Z\\to\\mathbb C$ is product\nmeasurable and\n$\\int |h(\\theta,x,z)|p(\\theta,x,z)\\,d(\\nu\\otimes\\mu)<\\infty$ for a fixed\n$\\theta$, Fubini permits exchanging the latent and observed integrals of\n$hp$. No exchange is claimed without this absolute integrability.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "alg-expectation-maximization",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**Inputs.** Supply a measurable parameter space $(\\Theta,\\mathcal T)$, sigma-finite measure spaces $(\\mathsf X,\\mathcal A,\\nu)$ and $(\\mathsf Z,\\mathcal C,\\mu)$, and a jointly measurable complete-data density $p(\\theta,x,z)$ relative to $\\nu\\otimes\\mu$ that integrates to one on each parameter fiber, as in [[def-latent-variable-joint-and-observed-data-factorization]]. This joint measurability is supplied explicitly and is not inferred from separate likelihood-density versions ([[def-likelihood-density]]). Also supply fixed observed data $x$, an initial value $\\theta_0$, a mode `exact` or `generalized`, and a candidate selector that returns either a candidate or `unavailable` in finite time at each call. Require $0<m_{\\theta_0}(x)<\\infty$ and\n$$\\int q_{\\theta_0}^x(z)\\,|\\log p(\\theta_0,x,z)|\\,\\mu(dz)<\\infty.$$\n\nWrite\n$$m_\\theta(x):=\\int_{\\mathsf Z}p(\\theta,x,z)\\,\\mu(dz),\\qquad q_\\theta^x(z):=\\frac{p(\\theta,x,z)}{m_\\theta(x)}$$\nonly when $0<m_\\theta(x)<\\infty$. By [[thm-observed-density-is-latent-marginal-under-tonelli]], $m_\\theta$ is the observed-data density and $q_\\theta^x$ is a probability density on every such fiber. The observed log likelihood is $\\ell_x(\\theta):=\\log m_\\theta(x)$ on those fibers.\n\nAt each valid iterate $\\theta_t$, set $q_t:=q_{\\theta_t}^x$ and define\n$$Q(\\theta\\mid\\theta_t):=\\int_{\\mathsf Z}\\log p(\\theta,x,z)\\,q_t(z)\\,\\mu(dz)$$\nonly for candidates where this integral is finite. Use $\\log0=-\\infty$ and set $q_t|\\log p|=0$ wherever $q_t=0$. The Q integral itself is choice-free. To test finite KL when $q_t\\mu\\ll q_\\theta^x\\mu$, assume AC as required by [[def-axiom-of-choice]] and [[def-kullback-leibler-divergence]]; when absolute continuity fails, $D=+\\infty$ by that definition without AC. An **admissible candidate** $\\theta$ also satisfies $0<m_\\theta(x)<\\infty$, $q_t\\mu\\ll q_\\theta^x\\mu$, and\n$$D(q_t\\mu\\|q_\\theta^x\\mu)<\\infty,$$\nwhere the direction of $D$ is that of [[def-kullback-leibler-divergence]]. Require $\\int q_t|\\log p(\\theta,x,\\cdot)|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$ for each candidate and $\\int q_t|\\log p(\\theta_t,x,\\cdot)|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$ for the current iterate.\n\n**Output.** Emit the iterate sequence beginning with $\\theta_0$. If a stopping condition occurs after $T$ successful updates, return the finite list $(\\theta_0,\\ldots,\\theta_T)$ and a status recording the first unavailable condition or selector result; do not append a failed candidate. If no stopping condition occurs, the emitted sequence is infinite and there is no finite stopping status.\n\n**Procedure.** For $t=0,1,2,\\ldots$, check that $0<m_{\\theta_t}(x)<\\infty$; if not, return the current list with status `stopped: invalid current marginal`. Form $q_t$ and $Q(\\cdot\\mid\\theta_t)$ on admissible candidates. If $Q(\\theta_t\\mid\\theta_t)$ is not finite, return with status `stopped: current Q is not finite`. Call the supplied selector. In **exact mode**, its candidate set must consist of admissible candidates and contain $\\theta_t$; it returns an attained maximizer of $Q$ on that set, or `unavailable` if no attained maximum is available. In **generalized mode**, it returns any admissible $\\theta_{t+1}$ with $Q(\\theta_{t+1}\\mid\\theta_t)\\ge Q(\\theta_t\\mid\\theta_t)$, or `unavailable` if no such update is supplied. If the selector returns `unavailable`, return with status `stopped: no required update supplied`; otherwise append its candidate and continue. The procedure asserts no convergence or global-optimality property and makes no assertion for a candidate that loses current latent support.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-em-observed-log-likelihood-is-nondecreasing",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $p(\\theta,x,z)$ be a jointly measurable normalized complete-data density as in [[def-latent-variable-joint-and-observed-data-factorization]], and fix observed data $x$. Put $m_\\theta(x)=\\int p(\\theta,x,z)\\,\\mu(dz)$. Let $\\theta_t$ and $\\theta$ satisfy $0<m_{\\theta_t}(x),m_\\theta(x)<\\infty$. Define $q_t(z)=p(\\theta_t,x,z)/m_{\\theta_t}(x)$ and $q_\\theta^x(z)=p(\\theta,x,z)/m_\\theta(x)$. Assume\n$$q_t\\mu\\ll q_\\theta^x\\mu,\\qquad \\int q_t(z)\\bigl(|\\log p(\\theta_t,x,z)|+|\\log p(\\theta,x,z)|\\bigr)\\,\\mu(dz)<\\infty,$$\nand $D(q_t\\mu\\|q_\\theta^x\\mu)<\\infty$. Use $\\log0=-\\infty$ and set $q_t|\\log p|=0$ wherever $q_t=0$. Then, with $Q(\\theta\\mid\\theta_t)=\\int q_t(z)\\log p(\\theta,x,z)\\,\\mu(dz)$,\n$$\\ell_x(\\theta)-\\ell_x(\\theta_t) =Q(\\theta\\mid\\theta_t)-Q(\\theta_t\\mid\\theta_t) +D(q_t\\mu\\|q_\\theta^x\\mu).$$\nConsequently, any admissible update with $Q(\\theta\\mid\\theta_t)\\ge Q(\\theta_t\\mid\\theta_t)$ does not decrease $\\ell_x$. An exact M-step has this property when its maximum is attained over an admissible candidate set containing $\\theta_t$. No equality-of-parameter conclusion is asserted when the KL divergence is zero.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-gaussian-mixture-exact-m-step-maximizes-q",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $n,p,K$ be positive integers, let $x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\in\\mathbb R^p$ have affine span $\\mathbb R^p$ (equivalently, no nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^p$ makes $v^{\\mathsf T}x_i$ constant over all $i$), and let $\\theta=((\\pi_k,\\mu_k,\\Sigma_k))_{k=1}^K$ be a finite Gaussian-mixture parameter with every $\\pi_k>0$ and every $\\Sigma_k$ symmetric positive definite. Define\n\n$$\\gamma_{ik}=\\frac{\\pi_k\\phi_p(x_i;\\mu_k,\\Sigma_k)}{\\sum_{j=1}^K\\pi_j\\phi_p(x_i;\\mu_j,\\Sigma_j)},\\qquad N_k=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\gamma_{ik},\\qquad \\bar x_k=\\frac1{N_k}\\sum_{i=1}^n\\gamma_{ik}x_i,$$\n\nand\n\n$$S_k=\\frac1{N_k}\\sum_{i=1}^n\\gamma_{ik}(x_i-\\bar x_k)(x_i-\\bar x_k)^{\\mathsf T}.$$\n\nFor a candidate parameter $\\vartheta=((\\rho_k,m_k,V_k))_{k=1}^K$ in the same domain, let\n\n$$Q(\\vartheta\\mid\\theta)=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\sum_{k=1}^K\\gamma_{ik}\\log\\!\\left(\\rho_k\\phi_p(x_i;m_k,V_k)\\right).$$\n\nThen every $N_k$ is positive, $\\sum_kN_k=n$, every $S_k$ is positive definite, and the unique global maximizer of $Q(\\vartheta\\mid\\theta)$ over all positive mixture weights summing to one, means in $\\mathbb R^p$, and symmetric positive-definite covariances is\n\n$$\\rho_k^*=N_k/n,\\qquad m_k^*=\\bar x_k,\\qquad V_k^*=S_k.$$\n\nIn particular, the displayed E-step responsibilities and M-step update formulas are well-defined and attain this maximum.",
          "uses": [
            "4.2",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-kullback-leibler-divergence",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $P,Q$ be probability measures on the same measurable space\n([[def-probability-measure]]). Absolute continuity $P\\ll Q$ has the convention\nof [[def-absolutely-continuous-with-respect-to-a-positive-measure]]. If\n$P\\not\\ll Q$, define $D(P\\|Q)=+\\infty$. If $P\\ll Q$, assume AC\n([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) as in [[def-likelihood-ratio-as-rn-derivative]] and\nlet $r=dP/dQ$ be a finite-valued nonnegative measurable representative of its\nRadon–Nikodym class; such a representative exists by\n[[lem-radon-nikodym-density-of-a-finite-positive-measure-has-a-nonnegative-representative]].\nOn $\\{r=0\\}$ set $\\ell=0$, and on $\\{r>0\\}$ set $\\ell=\\log r$. This\nfunction is measurable: for $a\\ge0$, $\\{\\ell>a\\}=\\{r>\\exp(a)\\}$, and for\n$a<0$, $\\{\\ell>a\\}=\\{r=0\\}\\cup\\{r>\\exp(a)\\}$, by the inverse and\nmonotonicity properties of the natural logarithm\n([[def-natural-logarithm]], [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]]). These are\nmeasurable sets by [[def-extended-real-valued-measurable-function]]. Since\n$P(\\{r=0\\})=\\int_{\\{r=0\\}}r\\,dQ=0$ by\n[[thm-likelihood-ratio-change-of-measure]], the value chosen there does not\naffect $\\int\\ell\\,dP$. Define\n$$D(P\\|Q):=\\int \\ell\\,dP,$$\nwhere $\\log$ is the natural logarithm ([[def-natural-logarithm]]).\n\nThe definition is independent of the representative: two representatives\nagree $Q$-almost everywhere, hence $P$-almost everywhere. Put\n$$\\psi(0):=0,\\qquad \\psi(t):=t\\log t\\quad(t>0).$$\nThe function $\\psi$ is continuous at zero: for $s\\ge1$,\n$$0\\le \\frac{\\log s}{s}=\\frac1s\\int_1^s\\frac{du}{u}\\le\\frac1s\\int_1^s\\frac{du}{\\sqrt u}\\le\\frac2{\\sqrt s}.$$\nThus $t\\log t\\to0$ as $t\\downarrow0$; here $L=\\log$ by\n[[cor-integral-logarithm-agrees-with-natural-logarithm]] and\n[[def-integral-logarithm]]. On $(0,\\infty)$, $\\psi$ is a product of continuous\nfunctions by [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]] and\n[[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]], so $\\psi$ is continuous on\n$[0,\\infty)$ and $\\psi(r)$ is measurable by\n[[def-extended-real-valued-measurable-function]]. Its signed integral is\ndefined by the positive and negative parts\n([[def-positive-and-negative-parts-of-a-function]],\n[[def-nonnegative-lebesgue-integral]]).\n\nFor $0<t\\le1$, the function $-t\\log t$ has derivative $-\\log t-1$ and\nmaximum $1/e$ at $t=1/e$: $e=\\exp(1)>1$ by\n[[def-real-exponential-function-and-e]] and\n[[thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing]], $1/e=\\exp(-1)$ by\n[[cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity]], and $\\log(1/e)=-1$ by\n[[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]] and the inverse definition. The mean value\ntheorem gives increase before and decrease after this point\n([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[thm-integral-logarithm-derivative-and-normalisation]],\n[[cor-integral-logarithm-agrees-with-natural-logarithm]], [[cor-mean-value-theorem]]).\nAt $t=0$ the negative part is zero. Since $Q$ is a probability measure,\n$$\\int \\psi(r)^-\\,dQ\\le\\frac1e.$$\nThe change-of-measure identity of [[thm-likelihood-ratio-change-of-measure]],\napplied to $\\ell^-$ gives $\\int\\ell^-\\,dP=\\int\\psi(r)^-\\,dQ\\le1/e$,\nso $\\int\\ell\\,dP$ is well-defined; applying it to both positive and negative\nparts gives\n$$D(P\\|Q)=\\int\\ell\\,dP=\\int\\psi(r)\\,dQ=\\int r\\log r\\,dQ,$$\nwith $0\\log0:=0$ and a finite negative part. Therefore the extended integral\nis well-defined without an $\\infty-\\infty$ ambiguity.\n\nThis convention is directional: the first argument must be absolutely\ncontinuous with respect to the second for the integral formula to apply.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-finite-sigma-finite-and-semifinite-measures",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space ([[def-measure-space]]).\n\n- The measure $\\mu$ is **finite** if $\\mu(X)<+\\infty$.\n- It is **sigma-finite** if there is a sequence $(E_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ in $\\mathcal A$ such that $X=\\bigcup_nE_n$ and $\\mu(E_n)<+\\infty$ for every $n$.\n- It is **semifinite** if, whenever $E\\in\\mathcal A$ and $\\mu(E)>0$, there is $F\\in\\mathcal A$ with $F\\subseteq E$ and $0<\\mu(F)<+\\infty$.\n\nThe last condition is automatic when $0<\\mu(E)<+\\infty$, by taking $F=E$; its substantive case is $\\mu(E)=+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces. Then:\n\n1. the set function $E \\mapsto (\\mu \\times \\nu)(E)$ of\n   [[def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces]] is a measure on\n   $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$;\n2. for measurable rectangles,\n   $$(\\mu \\times \\nu)(A \\times B)=\\mu(A)\\nu(B),$$\n   with the nonnegative extended-product convention $0\\cdot\\infty=\\infty\\cdot0=0$;\n3. the measure $\\mu \\times \\nu$ is sigma-finite; and\n4. it is the unique measure on $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$ with the\n   rectangle formula.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "lem-ac-supplies-sequential-choices-for-probability-constructions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Then countable choice holds. Moreover, if $R\\subseteq X\\times X$ is serial on a nonempty set X and $a\\in X$, there is a sequence $(x_n)_{n\\ge0}$ with $x_0=a$ and $x_n R x_{n+1}$ for every n. Thus the countable and dependent choices required by the probability-product suppliers are available under AC.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "thm-lebesgue-product-measure-agrees-with-euclidean-lebesgue-on-borel-sets",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$m,n \\ge 1$. Under the identification\n$$\\mathbb R^{m+n}=\\mathbb R^m \\times \\mathbb R^n,$$\nthe product measure $\\lambda_m \\times \\lambda_n$ and the Euclidean Lebesgue\nmeasure $\\lambda_{m+n}$ agree on every Borel subset of $\\mathbb R^{m+n}$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "def-product-sigma-algebra-and-finite-product-sigma-algebras",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B)$ be measurable spaces. Their\n**product sigma-algebra** is\n\n$$\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B := \\sigma(\\{A \\times B : A \\in \\mathcal A,\\ B \\in \\mathcal B\\}),$$\n\nthe sigma-algebra on $X \\times Y$ generated by the measurable rectangles of\n[[def-measurable-rectangle]].\n\nFor the empty family, put $\\prod_{i<0}X_i:=\\{()\\}$ and define its **empty\nproduct sigma-algebra** by\n\n$$\\bigotimes_{i<0}\\mathcal A_i:=\\{\\varnothing,\\{()\\}\\}.$$\n\nFor a nonempty finite family of measurable spaces $(X_i,\\mathcal A_i)$ with\n$i<n$, the **finite product sigma-algebra** is defined recursively by\n\n$$\\bigotimes_{i<1}\\mathcal A_i := \\mathcal A_0,\\qquad \\bigotimes_{i<n+1}\\mathcal A_i := \\left(\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\mathcal A_i\\right)\\otimes\\mathcal A_n.$$\n\nThis recursive definition is the finite base case used later when the library\npasses to countable product constructions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F14",
          "source": "cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $n,m\\ge1$. Every continuous map\n$f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ is Borel measurable in\nthe sense of [[def-borel-and-lebesgue-measurable-function-on-rn]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F15",
          "source": "cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space and let $T:V\\to V$ be\nself-adjoint. Then $V$ has an orthonormal basis consisting of eigenvectors of\n$T$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F15",
          "source": "thm-determinant-differential-and-jacobis-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A$ be an $n\\times n$ matrix and let $H$ be a direction matrix. Then\n\n$$D\\det(A)[H]=\\operatorname{tr}(\\operatorname{adj}(A)H).$$\n\nIf $A$ is invertible, then\n\n$$D\\det(A)[H]=\\det(A)\\operatorname{tr}(A^{-1}H).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F15",
          "source": "thm-derivative-of-matrix-inversion",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A$ be invertible. Then the inversion map is real Fr\\'echet differentiable\nat $A$, and for every direction $H$,\n\n$$D\\operatorname{inv}(A)[H]=-A^{-1}HA^{-1}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F16",
          "source": "thm-borel-products-of-euclidean-spaces-are-euclidean-borel",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m,n \\ge 1$. Under the identification\n$$\\mathbb R^{m+n}=\\mathbb R^m \\times \\mathbb R^n,$$\none has\n$$\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^m)\\otimes\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^n) = \\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^{m+n}).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F17",
          "source": "def-measure-with-density",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let\n$f:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be measurable. The **measure with density $f$ relative\nto $\\mu$** is the measure\n$$f\\,d\\mu:\\mathcal A\\to[0,+\\infty],\\qquad (f\\,d\\mu)(A):=\\int_A f\\,d\\mu,$$\nwhose measure property is supplied by\n[[thm-indefinite-integral-of-a-nonnegative-function-is-a-measure]].",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F17",
          "source": "def-probability-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **probability measure** on a measurable space $(X,\\mathcal A)$ is a measure $\\mathbb P$ with $\\mathbb P(X)=1$ ([[def-measure]]). The triple $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mathbb P)$ is a **probability space** ([[def-measure-space]]); $X$ is its sample space and the members of $\\mathcal A$ are its events.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F18",
          "source": "cor-additivity-of-the-nonnegative-lebesgue-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. If $f,g:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ are measurable, then\n$$\\int(f+g)\\,d\\mu=\\int f\\,d\\mu+\\int g\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F19",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ and let $a_0, \\dots, a_{n-1}$ and $b_0, \\dots, b_{n-1}$ be\nreals, with finite sums as in [[def-finite-sum]]. Then\n\n$$\\Big(\\sum_{k<n} a_k b_k\\Big)^{2} \\;\\le\\; \\Big(\\sum_{k<n} a_k^{2}\\Big)\\Big(\\sum_{k<n} b_k^{2}\\Big),$$\n\nand, in root form ([[thm-of-square-roots]]),\n\n$$\\Big|\\sum_{k<n} a_k b_k\\Big| \\;\\le\\; \\sqrt{\\sum_{k<n} a_k^{2}} \\; \\sqrt{\\sum_{k<n} b_k^{2}}.$$\n\nEquality holds in the first display if and only if the two lists are\nproportional, in the symmetric sense that there is a pair\n$(\\lambda, \\mu) \\ne (0,0)$ of reals with $\\lambda a_k = \\mu b_k$ for every\n$k < n$.\n\n**No root is used in the proof of the squared form.** That form is an identity\nplus a sign argument in the ordered field, and the root form is only a\nrestatement of it through the monotonicity of squaring on the nonnegatives\n([[lem-of-square-monotone]]); the root enters nowhere but the last step, where\nthat restatement is made and [[thm-of-square-roots]] is what supplies the\nsquare-root symbol. This matters here, because it makes the squared\ninequality independent of the existence theorem for roots.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "d-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $\\Theta_K$ be the set of mixture parameters in [F1], let $Y=(\\mathbb R^p)^n$ with product Lebesgue measure $\\nu=\\lambda_p^{\\otimes n}$, and let $Z=\\{1,\\ldots,K\\}^n$ with counting measure $\\#(B):=|B|$ for $B\\subseteq Z$. The parameter domain is Borel: the positive-weight simplex is relatively open in its affine hyperplane, and the SPD cone is open as follows. For a symmetric positive-definite matrix $A$, the spectral theorem [F15] gives an orthonormal eigenbasis. Every eigenvalue $\\lambda$ has a unit eigenvector $u$ with $\\lambda=u^{\\mathsf T}Au>0$, so the least eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\min}(A)$ is positive. For a symmetric perturbation $E$, define $\\|E\\|_F:=(\\sum_{i,j}e_{ij}^2)^{1/2}$. Applying [F19] to the finite lists $(e_{ij})$ and $(v_iv_j)$ gives $|v^{\\mathsf T}Ev|\\le\\|E\\|_F(\\sum_{i,j}v_i^2v_j^2)^{1/2}=\\|E\\|_F\\|v\\|_2^2$. Therefore, whenever $\\|E\\|_F<\\lambda_{\\min}(A)$, $$v^{\\mathsf T}(A+E)v\\ge(\\lambda_{\\min}(A)-\\|E\\|_F)\\|v\\|_2^2>0$$ for every nonzero $v$, so $A+E$ is positive definite. This supplies a relative open neighborhood of each $A$ in the symmetric-matrix subspace. For each fixed $z\\in Z$, the formula below is continuous in $(\\eta,y)\\in\\Theta_K\\times Y$: determinants and inverses are continuous on the SPD cone by [F15], and the remaining operations are sums, products, and exponentials. A continuous map on this Borel subspace has Borel preimages relative to it; those relative Borel sets are ambient Borel. The product-Borel identification for Euclidean factors [F16] and finite discreteness of $Z$ then make the function jointly measurable for the required product sigma-algebra.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F10",
            "F13",
            "F14",
            "F15",
            "F16",
            "F19",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-2-1",
          "claim": "Define the complete-data density, relative to $\\nu$ times counting measure on $Z$, by $p(\\eta,y,z):=\\prod_{i=1}^n\\pi_{z_i}\\phi_p(y_i;\\mu_{z_i},\\Sigma_{z_i}).$ Repeated use of [F9] constructs the finite product measures, including $\\nu\\otimes\\#$, and preserves sigma-finiteness. AC supplies the countable-choice premise of [F11] by [F10]; repeated use of [F11] then identifies $\\nu=\\lambda_p^{\\otimes n}$ with Euclidean Lebesgue measure on $\\mathbb R^{pn}$. For each component, [F1,F2] and finite-sum additivity [F18] give $\\int f_\\eta(y_i)\\,\\lambda_p(dy_i)=\\sum_k\\pi_k=1$. Distributing the finite sum over labels gives $\\sum_{z\\in Z}p(\\eta,y,z)=\\prod_i f_\\eta(y_i)$. Repeated Tonelli integration [F12] therefore gives $\\sum_{z\\in Z}\\int_Yp(\\eta,y,z)\\,\\nu(dy)=\\int_Y\\prod_i f_\\eta(y_i)\\,\\nu(dy)=\\prod_i\\int_{\\mathbb R^p}f_\\eta(y_i)\\,\\lambda_p(dy_i)=1.$ Thus $p$ is normalized on each parameter fibre as required by [F3].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "F11",
            "F12",
            "F18",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-3-1",
          "claim": "For a fixed observed sample $x_{1:n}$, summing the complete density over the finite label set yields $m_\\eta(x_{1:n})=\\sum_{z\\in Z}p(\\eta,x_{1:n},z)=\\prod_i\\sum_k\\pi_k\\phi_p(x_i;\\mu_k,\\Sigma_k)=\\prod_i f_\\eta(x_i).$ Every factor is finite and strictly positive by [F1,F2], so this marginal is positive and finite. Its logarithm is exactly the likelihood $\\ell(\\eta;x_{1:n})$ in the statement. By [F4], every normalized posterior $q_\\eta^{x_{1:n}}(z)=p(\\eta,x_{1:n},z)/m_\\eta(x_{1:n})$ is a probability mass function. More explicitly it is $q_\\eta^{x_{1:n}}(z)=\\prod_i\\gamma_{i,z_i}(\\eta),$ and every one of its finitely many masses is positive.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-4-1",
          "claim": "At any current parameter $\\theta$ and its exact update $\\theta'$, both posterior measures obtained from the probability masses in step 3.1 by [F17] are strictly positive on the same finite label set. Hence $q_\\theta^{x_{1:n}}\\ll q_{\\theta'}^{x_{1:n}}$, and the directional KL divergence is a sum of finitely many finite terms by [F8], so it is finite. For each $z$, $p(\\theta,x_{1:n},z)$ and $p(\\theta',x_{1:n},z)$ are strictly positive and finite; their logarithms are finite. The posterior expectations of the absolute log densities are finite sums and therefore finite. Thus every admissibility condition in [F5,F6] holds for this current/candidate pair.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F8",
            "F17",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-4-2",
          "claim": "The posterior in step 3.1 factors over observations. Taking the finite expectation of the complete log density gives $\\sum_{z\\in Z}q_\\theta^{x_{1:n}}(z)\\log p(\\vartheta,x_{1:n},z)=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\sum_{k=1}^K\\gamma_{ik}(\\theta)\\log\\!\\left(\\rho_k\\phi_p(x_i;m_k,V_k)\\right)=Q(\\vartheta\\mid\\theta),$ where $Q$ is the function in [F7]. Indeed, expand $\\log p$ as the sum over $i$, sum first over $z_i=k$, and use $\\sum_{z_j=1}^K\\gamma_{j,z_j}(\\theta)=1$ for every $j\\ne i$. This is a finite-sum identity, and all terms are finite by step 3.1.",
          "step": "4.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F7",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-5-1",
          "claim": "The local exact M-step result [F7] says $\\theta'$ globally maximizes this same Q over the full mixture domain. Step 4.1 verifies that both $\\theta$ and $\\theta'$ are admissible, so the two-point candidate set $\\{\\theta,\\theta'\\}$ is admissible and contains the current parameter; the global maximizer $\\theta'$ is therefore an attained maximizer over this set as required by the exact mode of [F5]. Consequently $Q(\\theta'\\mid\\theta)\\ge Q(\\theta\\mid\\theta)$. Applying the identity and nonnegativity conclusion of [F6], with the verified conditions from step 4.1, yields $\\ell(\\theta';x_{1:n})-\\ell(\\theta;x_{1:n})=Q(\\theta'\\mid\\theta)-Q(\\theta\\mid\\theta)+D(q_\\theta^{x_{1:n}}\\|q_{\\theta'}^{x_{1:n}})\\ge0.$ Thus one exact update does not decrease observed log likelihood.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 4.1",
            "step 4.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-6-1",
          "claim": "Initially $\\theta_0$ is in the mixture domain by hypothesis. If $\\theta_t$ is in that domain, step 3.1 supplies positive finite marginals and positive responsibilities, and [F7] supplies the next positive weights and SPD covariances; hence $\\theta_{t+1}$ is again in the domain. Induction gives valid parameters through $T$, and step 5.1 applies at each $t<T$, proving every displayed likelihood inequality. This proves the claim without asserting strict increase or convergence.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F7",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 5.1",
            "induction"
          ]
        }
      ],
